\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Finite incidence data and real fields}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{A \newvocab{finite incidence presentation} consists of
\vocabk{finite sets}{ftip-000A} #{V} of vertices and #{E} of edges,
with source and head maps #{s,h:E\to V}. Vertices and edges are distinct
types of objects. An edge #{e} runs from #{s(e)} to #{h(e)}; loops
#{s(e)=h(e)} and distinct edges with the same endpoints are allowed.}

\p{A \newvocab{real vertex field} is a function #{f:V\to\mathbb R}.
Write #{\mathbb R^V} for the vector space of these functions, with pointwise
operations, and similarly #{\mathbb R^E} for edge functions. Their
\newvocab{counting inner products} are
##{\langle f,g\rangle_V=\sum_{v\in V}f(v)g(v),\qquad
   \langle a,b\rangle_E=\sum_{e\in E}a(e)b(e).}
These finite sums are bilinear and symmetric. A sum of squares vanishes
exactly when all its entries vanish, so they are positive definite,
including on the zero space when an indexing set is empty. The real field
values remain continuous even though the carrier is finite.}

\p{The \newvocab{incidence map} is the linear map
##{D:\mathbb R^V\longrightarrow\mathbb R^E,\qquad
   (Df)(e)=f(h(e))-f(s(e)).}
Linearity follows by substituting a linear combination of fields. In the
delta-function bases, where #{\delta_v(u)} is one for #{u=v} and zero
otherwise, its \newvocab{incidence matrix} has entries
##{B_{ev}=(D\delta_v)(e)
   =\mathbf 1_{v=h(e)}-\mathbf 1_{v=s(e)}.}
Thus #{(Bf)_e=\sum_v B_{ev}f(v)=f(h(e))-f(s(e))}.
Rows are indexed by edges and columns by vertices. A loop has a zero row;
reversing an edge exchanges its endpoints and negates its row.}

\p{For an oriented loopless graph this is the sign convention of
Spielman and Srivastava \citet{Section 2.1, p. 4}{spielman2009effective}.
The indicator difference explicitly extends it to loops. Their connected,
positively weighted graph assumptions are not needed to define #{D}.}

\p{A positive edge-weight function #{w:E\to\mathbb R_{>0}} is additional
data, represented in the edge basis by #{W=\operatorname{diag}(w(e))}.
The incidence presentation, the linear map #{D}, and its matrix #{B}
should be distinguished. If permutation matrices #{P_V,P_E} reorder
vertex and edge coordinates, then
##{f'=P_Vf,\qquad (Df)'=P_EDf,\qquad
   B'=P_EBP_V^{-1}.}
The last equality follows from #{B'f'=P_EBf} for every #{f}.
Changing coordinates represents the same map; choosing different weights
changes the supplied data.}

\p{For the counting inner product, Mathlib's
\leanref{EuclideanSpace} is the corresponding finite function space and
\leanref{PiLp.inner_apply} gives the sum of coordinate inner products.
These declarations concern the pairing, rather than the incidence
presentation as a whole.}
